{
 "slug": "exponent-rules",
 "title": "Exponent Rules: Product, Quotient, Zero and Negative Exponents",
 "seo_title": "Grade 8 Exponent Rules Worksheets: Product, Quotient, Power, Zero and Negative Exponents",
 "short_title": "Exponent rules",
 "domain": "Expressions & Equations",
 "standard_code": "8.EE.A.1",
 "standard_url": "https://www.thecorestandards.org/Math/Content/8/EE/A/1/",
 "standard_text": "Know and apply the properties of integer exponents to generate equivalent numerical expressions. For example, 3² × 3⁻⁵ = 3⁻³ = 1/3³ = 1/27.",
 "meta_desc": "Free Grade 8 exponent rules worksheets: multiply and divide powers with the same base, power of a power, zero and negative exponents, and evaluate expressions like 3² × 3⁻⁵. 100 questions with an answer key.",
 "key_big": "Same base: multiply → add exponents, divide → subtract, power of a power → multiply.",
 "key_sub": "Any nonzero number to the power 0 is 1. A negative exponent means 1 over the positive power.",
 "body_html": "<h2>The exponent rules</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Multiply, same base: add exponents</td><td>2<sup>3</sup> × 2<sup>4</sup> = 2<sup>7</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Divide, same base: subtract exponents</td><td>5<sup>6</sup> ÷ 5<sup>2</sup> = 5<sup>4</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Power of a power: multiply exponents</td><td>(3<sup>2</sup>)<sup>4</sup> = 3<sup>8</sup></td></tr><tr><td>Zero exponent</td><td>7<sup>0</sup> = 1</td></tr><tr><td>Negative exponent</td><td>2<sup>−3</sup> = 1 ÷ 2<sup>3</sup> = 1/8</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Find the value of 3<sup>2</sup> × 3<sup>−5</sup>.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Add the exponents: 2 + (−5) = −3.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>3<sup>−3</sup> = 1 ÷ 3<sup>3</sup> = 1 ÷ 27.</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 1/27</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Multiplying the exponents when you multiply: 2³ × 2⁴ is 2⁷, not 2¹².</li><li>Multiplying the bases: 2³ × 2⁴ is not 4⁷.</li><li>Thinking a negative exponent makes a negative number: 2⁻³ = 1/8, not −8.</li><li>Thinking 5⁰ = 0. It is 1.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What are the rules of exponents?",
   "a": "With the same base, add exponents when you multiply, subtract when you divide, and multiply when you raise a power to a power. a⁰ = 1 and a⁻ⁿ = 1/aⁿ."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why is any number to the zero power equal to 1?",
   "a": "Because aⁿ ÷ aⁿ = a⁰ by the quotient rule, and any nonzero number divided by itself is 1."
  },
  {
   "q": "Does a negative exponent make a number negative?",
   "a": "No. A negative exponent makes a fraction: 2⁻³ = 1/2³ = 1/8."
  },
  {
   "q": "Can I add exponents if the bases are different?",
   "a": "No. The rules only work for the same base. 2³ × 3⁴ cannot be combined into one power."
  }
 ],
